Introduction
Monitoring a prop firm account requires more than displaying balance and equity. The EA must determine how much of the permitted daily and overall loss has been used, how close the account is to a breach, how much progress has been made toward the profit target, and whether each of those calculations is actually valid when reported.
Part 2
established the persistent account settings and daily-session reference needed for this work. Part 3 now turns those stored references into runtime financial states. Daily drawdown uses the equity captured when the trader confirms the session baseline, overall drawdown uses the initial challenge balance under a static floor model, and profit target progress uses the current realized balance.
These references must remain separate because they answer different compliance questions. The daily rule belongs to the current trading day, while the overall rule remains anchored to the challenge configuration. The profit target is also evaluated independently because it measures realized growth rather than drawdown risk.
The models used here are deliberate choices for Prop Firm Guard, not universal prop firm formulas. Other firms may use different daily references, trailing or high-watermark drawdown, or equity-based target conditions.
Part 3 also introduces readiness-aware calculations. If a required reference is missing or invalid, the EA must report that the calculation is unavailable instead of treating it as safe.
By the end of this part, Prop Firm Guard will calculate and classify daily drawdown, static overall drawdown, and realized-balance profit target progress, then combine them into one readiness-aware account-level guard status.
Defining Readiness-Aware Financial States
Part 3 needs separate runtime structures for daily drawdown, overall drawdown, and profit target progress because each rule uses a different reference and produces a different set of calculated values. Before adding those structures, we first extend the account-level guard states so the EA can represent an incomplete financial picture explicitly.
Add the following enumeration immediately after ENUM_TARGET_STATUS:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Overall guard status                                             |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Represents the combined state of the Part 3 financial rules.     |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
enum
ENUM_GUARD_STATUS
  {
   GUARD_NOT_READY =
0
,
// At least one required financial rule is unavailable
GUARD_SAFE,
// All evaluated financial rules are within safe limits
GUARD_TARGET_REACHED,
// Profit target is reached with no higher-priority risk
GUARD_WARNING,
// At least one drawdown rule is in warning state
GUARD_CRITICAL,
// At least one drawdown rule is in critical state
GUARD_BREACHED
// At least one drawdown rule has been breached
};
GUARD_NOT_READY is the important addition here. It gives the combined guard a way to distinguish an unavailable calculation from a valid safe result. Next, place the three financial-state structures immediately after
SDailySession
.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Daily drawdown state                                             |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Holds the current runtime result of the selected daily rule.     |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
struct
SDailyDrawdownState
  {
bool
ready;
// Whether the daily rule can be evaluated
double
referenceEquity;
// Confirmed equity reference for the session
double
currentEquity;
// Current live account equity
double
maximumDailyLoss;
// Maximum permitted daily loss
double
dailyEquityFloor;
// Minimum permitted equity for the daily rule
double
currentDailyLoss;
// Current loss from the daily equity reference
double
drawdownPercent;
// Loss as a percentage of reference equity
double
limitUsagePercent;
// Percentage of daily allowance already used
double
remainingAllowance;
// Amount remaining before daily breach
ENUM_COMPLIANCE_STATUS  status;
// Valid only when ready is true
};
The daily structure introduces three fields that are especially important for the revised model.
referenceEquity
stores the confirmed equity baseline used by the daily calculation,
dailyEquityFloor
stores the minimum equity permitted by that selected rule, and
ready
indicates whether the calculation is currently valid. A COMPLIANCE_SAFE value is meaningful only when
ready
is true. If the session has not been confirmed or the reference is invalid, the calculation is unavailable rather than safe.
Add the overall state next:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Overall drawdown state                                           |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Holds the current runtime result of the static overall rule.     |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
struct
SOverallDrawdownState
  {
bool
ready;
// Whether the overall rule can be evaluated
double
initialBalance;
// Fixed initial prop-firm account balance
double
currentEquity;
// Current live account equity
double
maximumOverallLoss;
// Maximum permitted overall loss
double
overallFloor;
// Static minimum permitted account equity
double
currentOverallLoss;
// Current loss from the initial balance
double
limitUsagePercent;
// Percentage of overall allowance already used
double
remainingAllowance;
// Amount remaining before overall breach
ENUM_COMPLIANCE_STATUS  status;
// Valid only when ready is true
};
This structure remains anchored to the initial challenge balance. Its
overallFloor
is static, so later account growth does not move that reference. The
ready
field again separates a valid calculated state from an unavailable one. Then add the profit target state:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Profit target state                                              |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Holds the current runtime result of the realized-balance target. |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
struct
SProfitTargetState
  {
bool
ready;
// Whether the target rule can be evaluated
double
initialBalance;
// Fixed initial prop-firm account balance
double
currentBalance;
// Current realized account balance
double
targetAmount;
// Profit required in account currency
double
targetBalance;
// Balance required to reach the target
double
currentProfit;
// Realized profit above the initial balance
double
progressPercent;
// Percentage of the target already achieved
double
remainingProfit;
// Profit still required to reach the target
ENUM_TARGET_STATUS  status;
// Valid only when ready is true
};
The same readiness rule applies here. TARGET_IN_PROGRESS, TARGET_NEARING, and TARGET_REACHED are valid only after the target calculation has succeeded. If the initial balance or target amount is invalid,
ready
remains false and the status must not be interpreted.
Finally, replace the current
global variables
block with:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Global variables                                                 |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
SAccountState         g_accountState;
// Latest monitored account state
SAccountSettings      g_accountSettings;
// Persisted prop-firm settings
SDailySession         g_dailySession;
// Current configured daily session
SDailyDrawdownState   g_dailyDrawdown;
// Current daily drawdown runtime state
SOverallDrawdownState g_overallDrawdown;
// Current overall drawdown runtime state
SProfitTargetState    g_profitTarget;
// Current profit-target runtime state
string
g_startBalanceEdit =
"PFG_StartBalanceEdit"
;
// Starting balance input field
string
g_confirmButton    =
"PFG_ConfirmBalance"
;
// Daily balance confirmation button
string
g_databaseName     =
"prop_firm_compliance.sqlite"
;
// SQLite database file
string
g_instanceLockName =
""
;
// Account-specific terminal instance lock
int
g_database             =
INVALID_HANDLE
;
// Active SQLite database handle
bool
g_hasDailySession      =
false
;
// Whether a daily session is currently loaded
bool
g_instanceLockAcquired =
false
;
// Whether this EA owns the instance lock
The distinction between these globals is important.
g_accountSettings
and
g_dailySession
contain state that originates from the persistence layer and can be restored after restart. By contrast,
g_dailyDrawdown
,
g_overallDrawdown
, and
g_profitTarget
are runtime calculation results. They are rebuilt from the stored references and the latest account values rather than stored as persistent records. This separation gives Part 3 a clear rule: persistence supplies the references, while the financial structures describe the latest calculated state.
Validating Financial Inputs and Classifying Loss Usage
Part 2 already validates the configured percentages and threshold ordering when the EA initializes, but the Part 3 calculation functions still need their own defensive checks. Runtime financial values can be unavailable, invalid, or unsuitable as denominators even when the original Inputs were valid. A monitoring function should therefore refuse to publish a financial status unless every value required by the calculation has passed validation.
The first helper provides a common test for financial references. Place
IsValidFinancialReference()
immediately after
UpdateAccountState()
:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Validate financial reference                                     |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
bool
IsValidFinancialReference(
const
double
value)
  {
//--- Financial references must be finite and strictly positive
if
(!
MathIsValidNumber
(value) ||
      value <=
0.0
)
return
(
false
);
return
(
true
);
  }
This helper is intended for values that will become financial references or divisors, such as the confirmed daily equity, the initial challenge balance, or the maximum permitted loss. A zero or negative value is not interpreted as zero risk. It makes the corresponding calculation unavailable.
The next helper converts valid drawdown usage into a compliance state. Add
ClassifyLossUsage()
immediately after
IsValidFinancialReference()
:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Classify consumed loss allowance                                 |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
bool
ClassifyLossUsage(
const
double
usagePercent,
                       ENUM_COMPLIANCE_STATUS  &status)
  {
//--- Reject invalid usage before assigning a compliance state
if
(!
MathIsValidNumber
(usagePercent) ||
      usagePercent <
0.0
)
     {
Print
(
"Prop Firm Guard: Cannot classify an invalid loss usage value."
);
return
(
false
);
     }
//--- Defend the classifier against invalid threshold configuration
if
(!
MathIsValidNumber
(InpWarningLevelPercent) ||
      !
MathIsValidNumber
(InpCriticalLevelPercent) ||
      InpWarningLevelPercent <=
0.0
||
      InpWarningLevelPercent >= InpCriticalLevelPercent ||
      InpCriticalLevelPercent >=
100.0
)
     {
Print
(
"Prop Firm Guard: Cannot classify loss usage with invalid warning thresholds."
);
return
(
false
);
     }
//--- Evaluate the most severe condition first
if
(usagePercent >=
100.0
)
     {
      status = COMPLIANCE_BREACHED;
return
(
true
);
     }
if
(usagePercent >= InpCriticalLevelPercent)
     {
      status = COMPLIANCE_CRITICAL;
return
(
true
);
     }
if
(usagePercent >= InpWarningLevelPercent)
     {
      status = COMPLIANCE_WARNING;
return
(
true
);
     }

   status = COMPLIANCE_SAFE;
return
(
true
);
  }
The order is important because the ranges overlap. A usage value of 100% is also above both the warning and critical thresholds, so the most severe condition must be checked first. The effective priority is therefore:
usage >=
100
-> BREACHED

usage >= critical
    -> CRITICAL

usage >= warning
    -> WARNING

otherwise
    -> SAFE
The function returns false when the usage value or threshold configuration is invalid. This prevents an unusable denominator from being converted into 0% usage and then misclassified as SAFE. The three runtime states also need deterministic reset helpers. Add them immediately after
ClassifyLossUsage()
:
